CREATED:20260825T173805Z
DESCRIPTION:<p>A central feature of Caribbean poetics has been a pictorial
  mode of representing the natural landscape and the complex multicultural
  societies that have taken root in the archipelago.  Verbal and visual ar
 ts converge in the creation of entangled ways of sensing and portraying t
 he vulnerable yet resilient terrain of human interaction.</p>\n<p>The Fra
 nklin Humanities Institute assembles an outstanding group of poets\, arti
 sts\, and scholars for a wide-ranging conversation about the interconnect
 ions between verbal depiction and visual aesthetics in the portrayal of t
 he natural and socio-cultural worlds.</p> \n<p>The Caribbean offers a par
 ticularly fecund eco-site from which to engage in such a conversation. Se
 a and land\, plantation and garden\, hurricane and drought\, cultivation 
 and extraction\, human and more-than-human life have long shaped the regi
 on's social histories and imaginative practices. Several generations of l
 eading Caribbean poets have continually re-imagined the terms through whi
 ch landscape\, ecology\, embodiment\, memory\, violence\, beauty\, and su
 rvival can be perceived\, represented and understood.<p>\n<p>Across a day
  and a half of presentations and conversations\, the symposium addresses 
 the question of how poetry in concert with the visual arts might help us 
 to view these relations from an entangled perspective. How do poetic and 
 visual practices intersect to register the histories deeply layered in Ca
 ribbean landscapes and seascapes? What might Caribbean poetics contribute
  to our thinking about the planetary ecological crisis\, racialized histo
 ries\, and possibilities for living otherwise?</p>\n<p><ul>Speakers:\n<li
 >Canisia Lubrin (University of Guelph)</li>\n<li>Lizabeth Paravisini-Gebe
 rt (Vassar College)</li>\n<li>Ishion Hutchinson (Cornell University)</li>
 \n<li>Maria Cristina Fumagalli (University of Essex)</li>\n<li>Mark McMor
 ris (Georgetown University)</li>\n<li>Kei Miller (University of Miami)</l
 i>\n<li>Manuel Arturo Abreu (Independent Researcher-Washington)</li>\n</u
 l></p>\n<p><a href="https://duke.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_9HydtPwCTQP9V2
 u">RSVP here</a></p>
DURATION:PT4H
