CREATED:20251007T015211Z
DESCRIPTION:Special screening:\n\n"About Face: Disrupting Ballet"\n(Jennif
 er Lin\, 2025\, 60 min\, USA\, English\, DCP)\n\nABOUT FACE: DISRUPTING B
 ALLET is a feature-length documentary that spotlights Georgina Pazcoguin 
 and Phil Chan\, two friends and NYC dancers of Asian descent\, who are ch
 allenging ballet companies to rid The Nutcracker and other ballets of off
 ensive racial stereotypes. What started as a pledge on a website has grow
 n into a global movement\, disrupting the elite world of ballet.\n\nGina\
 , a soloist with the New York City Ballet\, and Phil\, an author\, produc
 er and choreographer\, launch the global "Final Bow for Yellowface" movem
 ent [https://www.yellowface.org]. They post an online pledge and ask danc
 ers to jettison offensive "yellowface" caricatures from ballets like The 
 Nutcracker and La Bayadère. Hundreds from around the world sign the pledg
 e.\n\nWe watch in real time as the Scottish Ballet updates its version of
  the Chinese dance to both high praise and stinging backlash.\n\nPhil tak
 es on a bigger challenge of reimagining a full-length ballet: the problem
 atic La Bayadére. Working with dancers at Indiana University\, he boldly 
 transports the story from ancient India to a Hollywood studio in the 1920
 s.\n\nABOUT FACE vividly shows the tension and risks the two artists endu
 re as they push an art form-created for the entertainment of kings and qu
 eens hundreds of years ago-to more authentically reflect the American exp
 erience today.\n\n"[O]ne thing unites myself\, Jennifer Lin\, and the dan
 cers in the film: we are all of mixed-race Asian ancestry\, navigating a 
 duality that brings up persistent questions of identity and belonging. Li
 n's film offers catharsis\, modeling attentive care that asks ballet to c
 onfront itself honestly\, while also opening space for dialogue about rep
 resentation. And for me\, the film raises a difficult question: how do yo
 u love something that has also caused harm?" -- Lauren Berlin\, thINKingD
 ANCE \n\n==\nAbout the filmmaker:\n\nJennifer Lin spent a lifetime as a r
 eporter before turning to filmmaking a decade ago. During 32 years at The
  Philadelphia Inquirer\, she served as a financial correspondent on Wall 
 Street\, a national correspondent in Washington\, D.C.\, and an Asia corr
 espondent in Beijing. Her first documentary\, BEETHOVEN IN BEIJING\, expl
 ored the Philadelphia Orchestra's legacy in China and subsequent films\, 
 TEN TIMES BETTER and ABOUT FACE: DISRUPTING BALLET\, the examine the Asia
 n American experience in ballet.
DTSTAMP:20260903T195915Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T150000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260903T195915Z
