 olle
CREATED:20260911T141933Z
DESCRIPTION:Biofilm dispersion is a regulated developmental transition tha
 t enables bacteria to exit surface‑attached communities and re‑enter a pl
 anktonic state. This seminar will examine the molecular circuitry that dr
 ives dispersion\, with emphasis on the environmental cues\, signaling pat
 hways\, and phosphodiesterase networks that coordinate matrix degradation
  and cell release. Emerging data reveal that dispersed cells represent a 
 transcriptionally distinct physiological state. Their unique phenotype\, 
 including heightened susceptibility\, increased motility\, enhanced disse
 mination potential\, immune evasion\, and elevated virulence traits\, wil
 l be discussed. These features have important clinical implications\, pos
 itioning dispersed cells as key contributors to biofilm persistence\, dis
 semination\, and the establishment of secondary infections\, while also h
 ighlighting dispersion as a therapeutically relevant intervention point f
 or biofilm control.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20260911T141933Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260911T141933Z
LOCATION;X-BEDEWORK-UID=00f1fcdb-0f068baf-010f-068baf6e-00000000:None
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:CEE Seminar - Biofilm Dispersion: Regulatory Mechanisms\, Emergent
  Phenotypes\, and Clinical Consequences
